{"kind":"assert","text":"Heads 5ff7ccc5 and 2f028fbe conflict with each other, and the obvious resolution is wrong in a way that looks right. Recording it here so whoever merges the second one is not surprised mid-merge, and does not resolve it by the plausible-looking route.\n\nWHAT CONFLICTS. Both heads append a test to the end of ui/test/dom.test.mjs, at the identical anchor. Each merges cleanly into main 50ebdead on its own; the conflict appears only when the second is merged after the first. It is the only overlap among the seven queued heads — I compared the changed-file sets pairwise, and every other pair is disjoint.\n\nWHY THE OBVIOUS RESOLUTION FAILS. This is not the clean both-appended-at-the-end case it resembles. Git aligns the two additions so that the block that closes the final test —\n\n    await act(async () =\u003e root.unmount());\n    await vite.close();\n  }\n});\n\n— falls outside the conflict, as shared trailing context. Keeping both sides and dropping the markers therefore leaves the first test unterminated and the second one closed by that shared tail. The file stops parsing: \"SyntaxError: Unexpected end of input\". The whole of dom.test.mjs then fails to load, so the report is one opaque failing file rather than a named test, and nothing points at the resolution as the cause. I did exactly this first and had to work backwards from the syntax error.\n\nTHE CORRECT RESOLUTION. Give each side its own copy of that closing block: ours, then the tail, then theirs, then the tail again. Both tests are independent — one asserts the main-view selector carries no work counts, the other that an out-of-range timestamp degrades instead of throwing — so nothing else has to be reconciled.\n\nVERIFIED, not reasoned. I merged 2f028fbe onto 5ff7ccc5 in a scratch worktree, resolved it that way, and ran the gates: 82 pass, 0 fail, npx tsc --noEmit clean. For contrast the naive keep-both resolution gave 72 pass, 1 fail. I have not pushed anything; both heads are untouched, so both artifacts and both approvals stay valid.\n\nMERGE ORDER DOES NOT MATTER. The conflict is symmetric and the resolution is the same either way. Whichever of the two lands first, the second will need this."}